:{},"circuit_facts":[{"label":"kim_theta_0.0pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.2pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.35pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.5pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]}],"answer":"I ran one job on emerald:mock, which is IQM's free Emerald test device. The job ID is 01a1648b-0224-73c3-928d-d4d80bc4f220. It held 4 circuits with 250 shots each, and it completed. The test device returns made-up results. It does not simulate your circuit, so its numbers tell you nothing about the physics. The \"ideal\" column below is the real physics answer.\n\nWhat I built:\n- The circuit used all 54 qubits. Every qubit started in 0.\n- Each of the 3 time steps did two things. First, every qubit got an RX(theta) turn. Then every coupler got one RZZ(-pi/2) gate.\n- The real emerald lists 85 working couplers today. The mock lists 90, so I used only the 85 that the real chip lists.\n- RZZ(-pi/2) is the same gate as one CZ plus Z turns on both qubits. So every coupler got exactly one CZ per step. I moved the Z turns into the phases of later single-qubit turns. That is 255 CZ gates per circuit.\n- I wrote the circuits in IQM's native gates and sent them without a transpiler. No compiler step could add, merge, or drop a coupler gate.\n- I read the circuits back from the server. Each one has 255 CZs on all 85 couplers and measures all 54 qubits (QB1 to QB54).\n\nHow I got the ideal values: a qubit's value after 3 steps depends only on qubits at most 2 couplers away. That is at most 13 qubits, so I simulated each qubit's small neighborhood exactly, without noise. I checked this shortcut and my gate rewriting against a full simulation of a smaller 9-qubit grid. They agreed to 1e-15.\n\nMagnetization (the average of Z over all 54 qubits), ideal and then measured:\n- theta = 0: ideal +1.000, measured +0.007\n- theta = 0.2 pi: ideal +0.452, measured -0.011\n- theta = 0.35 pi: ideal +0.113, measured -0.030\n- theta = 0.5 pi: ideal 0.000, measured +0.016\n\nWhat a perfect quantum computer would show: with no sideways field, every spin stays up and the magnetization is 1. A stronger field tips the spins over, and the coupler gates spread that tipping across the sheet. So the magnetization drops to 0.45, then to 0.11, and at theta = 0.5 pi it is exactly 0, which means the order has fully melted. This shows that a sideways (transverse) field fights the Z order, and the order breaks down faster as the field gets stronger.\n\nThe measured numbers all sit near 0, and they do not change with the field. That comes from the test device's fake results, not from physics. A real Emerald QPU would follow the ideal curve, but noise would pull it toward 0. To see the curve on hardware you would run this same job on the real \"emerald\", which costs credits.\n\nThe counts list one entry per circuit (bitstrings run from QB1 on the left to QB54 on the right). The single counts field is left empty because this job has 4 circuits.\n\nFiles are in /Users/claytonchancey/Desktop/quantum/.claude/worktrees/wf_8d7e6233-d69-23/work (kim.py, dqa.json, result.json). The job ID is also in /Users/claytonchancey/Desktop/quantum/.claude/worktrees/wf_8d7e6233-d69-23/job_log.txt."}
